                <h2>This Week's Observation: The 'Hidden Bottleneck' in Agency Account Opening Surfaces</h2>
<p>This week, the Yiyu technical team discovered an interesting pattern while processing client reconciliations: over 40% of recharge delay cases didn't originate from payment issues, but from <strong>opaque agency account opening progress</strong>—operations teams didn't know if accounts were approved, finance teams didn't know which account to recharge, and ad teams didn't know if accounts could support new creatives.</p>
<p>This information gap is not uncommon among overseas companies. With frequent policy adjustments on platforms like TikTok Shop, Meta, and Google Ads, agency account opening response cycles have extended from the original 3-5 business days to 7-14 business days or even longer. A投放负责人 from a utility app told us: "We opened 12 accounts simultaneously, dealing with different agencies and AMs, exchanged over 20 emails, and still couldn't figure out which would be approved first."</p>
<p>When business scale expands from 3-5 accounts to 20+, management relying on spreadsheets, emails, and WeChat groups hits its ceiling. Systematic kanban fundamentally solves this problem.</p>
<h2>Kanban Transforms 'Uncertainty' into 'Trackable Nodes'</h2>
<p>Agency account opening is a typical multi-party collaboration process: client submits qualifications → agency initial review → platform submission → awaiting review → account activation → finance links recharge. Delays can occur at each stage, but traditional management methods make these nodes "disappear into emails."</p>
<p>The core value of systematic kanban is making these nodes <strong>visible, visualizable, and alertable</strong>. Specifically:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>All account statuses at a glance</strong>: which platform each account belongs to, current review stage, estimated completion time—all visible on the kanban;</li>
<li><strong>Automatic anomaly alerts</strong>: when an account stays at one stage longer than expected, the system automatically marks and notifies the responsible person, avoiding "delays discovered two weeks later";</li>
<li><strong>Multi-party collaboration transparency</strong>: agency AMs, finance reconcilers, and ad operations can all see the same kanban, reducing miscommunication from information asymmetry;</li>
<li><strong>Traceable historical records</strong>: complete records of each account's opening date, supplementary materials, and recharge history for subsequent audits and reviews.</li>
</ul>
<p>Yiyu backend data shows that clients adopting kanban management saw account status query response times drop from an average of 4.2 hours to within 0.5 hours, with recharge delay rates decreasing by approximately 60%.</p>
<h2>Actionable Recommendations for Overseas Teams This Week</h2>
<p>If your team is struggling with agency account opening management chaos, here are action items based on this week's observations:</p>
<p><strong>Short-term (achievable within 1-2 weeks)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Catalog existing accounts</strong>: consolidate account information scattered across emails, WeChat, and spreadsheets into one document, noting each account's platform, agency, submission date, and current status;</li>
<li><strong>Establish status classification standards</strong>: recommend dividing into "materials pending supplement," "under review," "approved pending activation," and "activated"—ensuring consistent status understanding across team members;</li>
<li><strong>Assign account status responsibility</strong>: designate someone to follow up and be responsible for each account, reducing vacuum zones where "everyone is waiting but no one is managing."</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Medium-term (optimizable within one month)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Introduce kanban tools</strong>: if your team manages 10+ accounts, consider using project management tools (like Feishu Multi-dimensional Tables, Notion) to build kanbans with core fields including account name, platform, agency, submission date, estimated completion date, current status, and alert flags;</li>
<li><strong>Establish status synchronization mechanisms with agencies</strong>: agree on status update frequency early in cooperation (e.g., every Monday and Thursday), avoiding passive waiting;</li>
<li><strong>Set recharge预留 windows</strong>: based on kanban predictions for account activation times, initiate recharge processes 3-5 days in advance to avoid ad interruption due to insufficient balance when accounts are ready.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Long-term (quarterly perspective)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Review opening cycle data</strong>: analyze which platforms have the longest review cycles and which agencies respond fastest, using this as a basis for future channel optimization;</li>
<li><strong>Integrate with agency systems</strong>: if business scale is sufficient, consider partnering with agencies like Yiyu to achieve API-level account status synchronization, further reducing manual tracking costs.</li>
</ol>
<h2>This Week's Summary</h2>
<p>The systematic kanban for agency account opening services is essentially a <strong>visibility management solution</strong>. It doesn't solve whether agencies respond quickly, but whether information can flow timely and accurately within your team. As overseas companies continue scaling ad investment, account growth isn't a linear problem but an exponentially complex one.</p>
<p>Kanban may not speed up agency review times, but it ensures you know <strong>what's happening, who's handling it, and when to follow up</strong>. This certainty is precisely the most scarce resource for overseas teams managing multi-platform, multi-account operations.</p>
<p>If you're troubled by opaque account opening progress, start with the short-term actions from this week's checklist—make information visible first, then optimize efficiency.</p>
